task: actor-reactive-bench (measurement; no library change unless a defect shows)
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-06
branch: feature/actor-reactive-bench

why: okay-actor and okay-reactive have no numbers at all. Both are
  built on the channel that was measured all day: an actor's mailbox
  IS a Channel, `tell` is `mailbox.send(m)` -- one Async.Await per
  message, the exact shape feed-offer-first just removed from the
  producer side -- and `ask` takes a Timer per call. The reactive
  bridge moves demand in half-window batches over a channel of 256.
  Optimising either blind would repeat today's mistakes; measuring
  them costs one lane.

lanes, JMH, compare/: actor tell throughput (N messages into one
  actor that sums, then a final ask to read the sum); actor ask
  round-trip (N sequential asks); actor spawn+stop cost; reactive
  round trip (okay Source -> publisher -> source -> runForeach, N
  elements) against a plain okay Source of the same N, so the bridge's
  price is a ratio, not a number.

what is recorded: the numbers, bytes/op, and a JFR class breakdown for
  the tell lane -- and a named next step only where the profile shows
  one. If tell's per-message Await dominates, the fix is offer-first on
  tell, which is a separate lane with its own laws.

note: compare's build dependencies may need okayActor.jvm and
  okayReactive added -- build.sbt is a file every agent touches; the
  edit is two tokens on one line and announced here.
